<!--
╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
║ PRISMA AIRS OAUTH TOKEN FRAGMENT                                            ║
║ Version: 1.0.0                                                               ║
║ Built for: Azure API Management (APIM)                                      ║
╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝

DESCRIPTION:
Fetches and caches a Prisma AIRS OAuth bearer token using a two-step flow:
  1. Exchange service account credentials against Strata Cloud Manager (SCM) auth
     to obtain a short-lived (15 min) SCM access token.
  2. Use the SCM token to call the AIRS management API and obtain a 3-hour
     AIRS OAuth token (requested via tokenTtlInterval=3&tokenTtlUnit=hours).

The AIRS token is cached in APIM's internal cache with a TTL derived from the
token's own expires_in (minus a safety margin) so it is shared across all
requests/sessions without hitting the auth endpoints on every call. When the
cache entry is missing, expired, or rejected by AIRS as invalid, a fresh token
is fetched and stored.

LIMITATION — APIM internal cache availability:
  cache-lookup-value / cache-store-value require APIM's internal cache, which
  is NOT available on the Consumption tier, and is not available on
  self-hosted gateways unless the cache add-on is configured. On those
  deployments every request pays the full two-hop token-fetch latency, since
  nothing persists across requests. There is currently no external-cache
  (e.g. Redis) fallback — if you're on one of these tiers/deployments, either
  accept the per-request fetch cost or wire in an external cache yourself.

After this fragment executes, the context variable `PrismaAirsToken` holds the
current AIRS OAuth token. The scan fragment (panw-airs-scan-v2.1.2) reads this
variable when PrismaAirsAPI == "OAUTH" and sets Authorization: Bearer accordingly.

NAMED VALUES REQUIRED (create these as Secret named values in APIM):
  - panw-client-id      SCM service account Client ID (used for Basic auth to SCM)
  - panw-client-secret  SCM service account Client Secret (used for Basic auth to SCM)
  - panw-tsg            Strata Cloud Manager TSG ID
  - panw-customer-app   The Application name exactly as defined in the AIRS API Application
                        definition — passed as "customer_app" in the AIRS OAuth body

REUSED NAMED VALUES (must already exist for the scan fragment):
  - airs-api            Your static AIRS API key — reused here as "client_id" in the AIRS
                        OAuth body (the field name is misleadingly named by AIRS; it expects
                        the API key, not the SCM service account Client ID)

NOTE ON panw-client-id vs AIRS client_id:
  The AIRS OAuth endpoint body field named "client_id" is misleadingly named —
  it is NOT the SCM service account Client ID (panw-client-id). It is the AIRS
  API key (airs-api), which is reused here to avoid duplicating named values.
  panw-customer-app must match the Application name exactly as registered in AIRS.
  UNVERIFIED: this behavior was observed, not confirmed against an authoritative
  AIRS reference. The pan.dev management API overview only documents using a
  client ID/secret to obtain a token, not this reuse of the static API key —
  confirm against https://pan.dev/ docs or with Palo Alto support before relying
  on it in a new deployment.

OAUTH FETCH FAILURES VS failOpen:
  Failures while acquiring the token itself (SCM auth, AIRS OAuth exchange) are
  treated as infrastructure/configuration errors, not as AIRS-availability
  errors, so they always return a hard 502 here regardless of the scan
  fragment's failOpen setting. This is intentional — failOpen governs what
  happens when AIRS is reachable but the *scan* call fails, not when the
  deployment is missing or misconfigured. Contrast with panw-airs-scan-v2.1.2,
  where a failed AIRS scan call does honor failOpen.

USAGE:
  Place this fragment in the inbound section BEFORE the scan fragment:

  <inbound>
      <set-variable name="PrismaAirsAPI" value="OAUTH" />
      <include-fragment fragment-id="panw-airs-oauth" />
      <include-fragment fragment-id="panw-airs-scan" />
  </inbound>

CACHING STRATEGY:
  APIM internal cache (cache-store-value / cache-lookup-value) is used rather
  than Named Values. Named Values are static configuration — they cannot be
  written at runtime. The internal cache persists across requests and gateway
  worker processes within an APIM instance, making it suitable for shared token
  state (subject to the Consumption-tier/self-hosted limitation noted above).
  TTL is derived from the expires_in field in the AIRS OAuth response, minus a
  1-hour safety buffer (falls back to the full requested 3-hour interval minus
  the same buffer if expires_in is absent or unparseable).

  On a 401/403 from AIRS (token revoked, rotated, or expired early), the scan
  fragment evicts this cache entry and re-includes this fragment once to fetch
  a fresh token before retrying — see panw-airs-scan-v2.1.2's AIRS call.

-->
<fragment>
	<!-- ============================================================================
     PHASE 0: LOAD SECRETS INTO VARIABLES
     ============================================================================ -->
	<!-- Named-value {{...}} substitution happens as plain-text replacement into
	     this XML before any C# expression below is compiled. Interpolating a
	     secret directly into a C# string literal (e.g. "{{panw-client-secret}}")
	     means a secret containing a double-quote or backslash would break policy
	     compilation or change the expression's meaning. Loading named values into
	     context.Variables here — via a plain XML attribute, which APIM's
	     templating safely substitutes into — avoids that: every C# expression
	     below reads a proper runtime string value instead of interpolated source
	     text. -->
	<set-variable name="panwClientId" value="{{panw-client-id}}" />
	<set-variable name="panwClientSecret" value="{{panw-client-secret}}" />
	<set-variable name="panwTsg" value="{{panw-tsg}}" />
	<set-variable name="panwCustomerApp" value="{{panw-customer-app}}" />
	<set-variable name="airsApiKeyForOAuth" value="{{airs-api}}" />
	<!-- Optional override for the AIRS management API host, mirroring
	     PrismaAirsEndpoint in panw-airs-scan-v2.1.2 — needed for non-US regions. -->
	<set-variable name="airsManagementHost" value="@{
            string host = context.Variables.GetValueOrDefault&lt;string&gt;(&quot;PrismaAirsManagementHost&quot;, null);
            return !string.IsNullOrEmpty(host) ? host : &quot;api.sase.paloaltonetworks.com&quot;;
        }" />
	<!-- ============================================================================
     PHASE 1: CACHE CHECK
     ============================================================================ -->
	<!-- Try to load a cached AIRS OAuth token. panw-tsg is a single, instance-wide
	     named value — this key does not support multiple TSGs sharing one
	     gateway; it only namespaces the cache entry in case other cache keys
	     exist on the same APIM instance. Multi-TSG-per-gateway would require
	     per-request TSG input, which this fragment does not support. -->
	<cache-lookup-value key="@(&quot;panw-airs-oauth-token-&quot; + (string)context.Variables[&quot;panwTsg&quot;])" variable-name="PrismaAirsToken" />
	<choose>
		<when condition="@(context.Variables.GetValueOrDefault&lt;string&gt;(&quot;PrismaAirsToken&quot;) != null)">
			<!-- Cache hit — token is still valid, nothing to do -->
		</when>
		<otherwise>
			<!-- Cache miss — fetch a fresh token via the two-step flow below -->
			<!-- ============================================================================
             PHASE 2: SCM ACCESS TOKEN (15-minute short-lived token)
             ============================================================================ -->
			<!-- Step 1: Exchange service account credentials for an SCM bearer token.
			     Uses HTTP Basic auth (client_id:client_secret) with form-encoded body. -->
			<send-request mode="new" response-variable-name="scmAuthResponse" timeout="15" ignore-error="true">
				<set-url>https://auth.apps.paloaltonetworks.com/oauth2/access_token</set-url>
				<set-method>POST</set-method>
				<set-header name="Content-Type" exists-action="override">
					<value>application/x-www-form-urlencoded</value>
				</set-header>
				<set-header name="Authorization" exists-action="override">
					<!-- Basic auth: base64(urlencode(client_id):urlencode(client_secret)),
					     per RFC 6749 §2.3.1 — form-urlencode each component before joining
					     with ":" and Base64-encoding, so a secret containing ":" or other
					     reserved characters can't be misread as a field separator. -->
					<value>@{
            string id = Uri.EscapeDataString((string)context.Variables["panwClientId"]);
            string secret = Uri.EscapeDataString((string)context.Variables["panwClientSecret"]);
            string encoded = Convert.ToBase64String(System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(id + ":" + secret));
            return "Basic " + encoded;
        }</value>
				</set-header>
				<set-body>@("grant_type=client_credentials&amp;scope=tsg_id:" + Uri.EscapeDataString((string)context.Variables["panwTsg"]))</set-body>
			</send-request>
			<!-- Validate SCM auth response -->
			<choose>
				<when condition="@(context.Variables.GetValueOrDefault&lt;IResponse&gt;(&quot;scmAuthResponse&quot;)?.StatusCode != 200)">
					<!-- SCM auth failed — return a clear error rather than a cryptic 500 -->
					<return-response>
						<set-status code="502" reason="Bad Gateway" />
						<set-header name="Content-Type" exists-action="override">
							<value>application/json</value>
						</set-header>
						<set-body>@{
                int code = context.Variables.GetValueOrDefault&lt;IResponse&gt;("scmAuthResponse")?.StatusCode ?? 0;
                return new JObject(
                    new JProperty("error", $"🛡️ PRISMA AIRS: SCM authentication failed (HTTP {code}). Check panw-client-id, panw-client-secret, and panw-tsg named values.")
                ).ToString(Newtonsoft.Json.Formatting.None);
            }</set-body>
					</return-response>
				</when>
			</choose>
			<!-- Extract SCM access_token from response -->
			<set-variable name="scmAccessToken" value="@{
            var body = context.Variables.GetValueOrDefault&lt;IResponse&gt;(&quot;scmAuthResponse&quot;).Body.As&lt;JObject&gt;(preserveContent: true);
            return body[&quot;access_token&quot;]?.ToString();
        }" />
			<choose>
				<when condition="@(string.IsNullOrEmpty(context.Variables.GetValueOrDefault&lt;string&gt;(&quot;scmAccessToken&quot;)))">
					<return-response>
						<set-status code="502" reason="Bad Gateway" />
						<set-header name="Content-Type" exists-action="override">
							<value>application/json</value>
						</set-header>
						<set-body>{"error": "🛡️ PRISMA AIRS: SCM auth response did not contain an access_token field."}</set-body>
					</return-response>
				</when>
			</choose>
			<!-- ============================================================================
             PHASE 3: AIRS OAUTH TOKEN (3-hour token)
             ============================================================================ -->
			<!-- Step 2: Use the SCM token to obtain a 3-hour AIRS OAuth token.
			     airs-api → "client_id" body field (misleadingly named by AIRS; reuses the
			     existing named value rather than duplicating it as panw-client-app).
			     panw-customer-app → "customer_app", must match the Application name exactly
			     as defined in the AIRS API Application definition. -->
			<send-request mode="new" response-variable-name="airsOauthResponse" timeout="15" ignore-error="true">
				<set-url>@("https://" + (string)context.Variables["airsManagementHost"] + "/aisec/v1/mgmt/oauth/client_credential/accesstoken?tokenTtlInterval=3&amp;tokenTtlUnit=hours")</set-url>
				<set-method>POST</set-method>
				<set-header name="Content-Type" exists-action="override">
					<value>application/json</value>
				</set-header>
				<set-header name="Accept" exists-action="override">
					<value>application/json</value>
				</set-header>
				<set-header name="Authorization" exists-action="override">
					<value>@("Bearer " + context.Variables.GetValueOrDefault&lt;string&gt;("scmAccessToken"))</value>
				</set-header>
				<set-body>@{
            return new JObject(
                new JProperty("client_id", (string)context.Variables["airsApiKeyForOAuth"]),
                new JProperty("customer_app", (string)context.Variables["panwCustomerApp"])
            ).ToString(Newtonsoft.Json.Formatting.None);
        }</set-body>
			</send-request>
			<!-- Validate AIRS OAuth response -->
			<choose>
				<when condition="@(context.Variables.GetValueOrDefault&lt;IResponse&gt;(&quot;airsOauthResponse&quot;)?.StatusCode != 200)">
					<return-response>
						<set-status code="502" reason="Bad Gateway" />
						<set-header name="Content-Type" exists-action="override">
							<value>application/json</value>
						</set-header>
						<set-body>@{
                int code = context.Variables.GetValueOrDefault&lt;IResponse&gt;("airsOauthResponse")?.StatusCode ?? 0;
                return new JObject(
                    new JProperty("error", $"🛡️ PRISMA AIRS: AIRS OAuth token fetch failed (HTTP {code}). Check airs-api / panw-customer-app named values and SCM service account permissions.")
                ).ToString(Newtonsoft.Json.Formatting.None);
            }</set-body>
					</return-response>
				</when>
			</choose>
			<!-- Extract AIRS token and TTL from response -->
			<set-variable name="airsOauthBody" value="@(context.Variables.GetValueOrDefault&lt;IResponse&gt;(&quot;airsOauthResponse&quot;).Body.As&lt;JObject&gt;(preserveContent: true))" />
			<set-variable name="PrismaAirsToken" value="@(context.Variables.GetValueOrDefault&lt;JObject&gt;(&quot;airsOauthBody&quot;)?[&quot;access_token&quot;]?.ToString())" />
			<choose>
				<when condition="@(string.IsNullOrEmpty(context.Variables.GetValueOrDefault&lt;string&gt;(&quot;PrismaAirsToken&quot;)))">
					<return-response>
						<set-status code="502" reason="Bad Gateway" />
						<set-header name="Content-Type" exists-action="override">
							<value>application/json</value>
						</set-header>
						<set-body>{"error": "🛡️ PRISMA AIRS: AIRS OAuth response did not contain an access_token field."}</set-body>
					</return-response>
				</when>
			</choose>
			<!-- ============================================================================
             PHASE 4: CACHE STORE
             ============================================================================ -->
			<!-- Derive cache TTL from expires_in (returned as a string of seconds), minus a
			     1-hour safety margin so the token is never used in its last hour. The
			     fallback when expires_in is absent/unparseable must not outlive the
			     token's real life: use the 3-hour interval we requested (10800s) minus
			     the same margin, rather than a fixed value that could exceed it. If even
			     that margin doesn't fit (a token shorter than 1h), cache briefly (5min)
			     instead of not caching at all. -->
			<set-variable name="airsOauthCacheTtl" value="@{
            const int marginSeconds = 3600;
            const int requestedTokenLifetimeSeconds = 3 * 3600;
            string raw = context.Variables.GetValueOrDefault&lt;JObject&gt;(&quot;airsOauthBody&quot;)?[&quot;expires_in&quot;]?.ToString();
            int lifetimeSeconds = requestedTokenLifetimeSeconds;
            if (int.TryParse(raw, out int expiresIn) &amp;&amp; expiresIn &gt; 0) {
                lifetimeSeconds = expiresIn;
            }
            int ttl = lifetimeSeconds - marginSeconds;
            return ttl &gt; 0 ? ttl : 300;
        }" />
			<cache-store-value key="@(&quot;panw-airs-oauth-token-&quot; + (string)context.Variables[&quot;panwTsg&quot;])" value="@(context.Variables.GetValueOrDefault&lt;string&gt;(&quot;PrismaAirsToken&quot;))" duration="@((int)context.Variables[&quot;airsOauthCacheTtl&quot;])" />
		</otherwise>
	</choose>
</fragment>
